> I'm trying to port a large Fortran program to alpha Linux using f2c/gcc.
> My system is a UDB running RedHat 5.1.  I'm able to compile and run my
> code, but I'm having some strange numerical problems.  I get floating
> point exceptions in sections of the code which evaluate exp(-x) for large
> positive values of x.

FAQ. You need to use the -mieee flag in order to use denormalized
numbers at all. Unfortunately, the current exp() and other routines
will crash otherwise; ideally they should underflow to zero if you do
not specify -mieee.

Note that -mieee will slow down your code by a factor of 2 or more.

> I'd like to just have these cases underflow to zero without generating
> exceptions, does anyone know how to make this happen?

As a workaround, you can figure out the smallest value for which exp()
does not crash, and explicitly test for it in your code.

-- g


