I believe the 264 is capable of examining on the order of
60 asm instructions to decide what to do. That will fix
most of the stalls one sees using 164's. I would expect
a 21164 optimized binary would run well on the 264. The
21164 binary would already have the non-variable stuff
removed from critical loops so it has reasonable inner
loops, at least from what I've seen comparing my FORTRAN
to it's generated asm. It's not perfect, but it isn't
bad and the ability to look that far ahead should do
wonders. Assuming egcs has 264 scheduling added, it 
will help to re-compile at that point.

Re-compile would also be necessary to get any new instructions
though. Still would need egcs support for them.


Wes


