With regards to your message at 03:00 PM 01-27-99 +0000, Phil Carmody.
Where you stated:
>Trent Gillespie wrote:
>> Just thought I'd let everyone know that someone at Alpha-Processor
>> "unofficially" recommended to no longer use the UX board, because it
>> will not be supported with new firmware, consoles or anything else in
>> the future.
>> 
>> On a good note, the DP264 (dual 21264) will be released Monday, the LX
>> is going back into reproduction, the RX board is likely scheduled for
>> production by the end of the quarter, and Compaq has a new 21264 based
>> machine debuting Monday...
>
>I've only just saved all my hard earned pennies and got myself a UX4
>based workstation (I assume the UX4 is a 'ruffian', I'm very new to
>all the terminology). Currently it only has NT4, but I intend (when
>I've worked out what the heck MILO/ARCBIOS/other-stuff is) to install
>RedHat as my primary operating system.
>Am I scuppered? 

No, you are not. There is "unsupported" as in "we are happy and not
changing it" and there is "unsupported" as in "bugger off".

In this case they will not be making any more UX boards ( there are
thousands in the warehouses) and no further updates are needed.

For that matter the LX falls in the same category. The only reason they are
making a few thousand more is that they are popular for clusters, and for
running UNIX. As there is SRM firmware available for the UX ( UX is not a
DEC/Copaq part but a Deskstation/Samsung/API part) the only choice is the
LX. SRM is needed to run UNIX, or to support remote booting over a network
via bootp. Still, they are not making _that_ big a quantity of LX, so it is
not much different..
In either case the chips used to make these boards, both UX and LX are
pretty well out of production, and there will be no further large runs of
them.

All the new development time energy and $$ are going into the DP264 and the
SP264. Some effort in the RX, as it is supposed to be a "low cost" Alpha.
We shall see...

>I thought the UX was the latest greatest 164 system - in which case
>how come it's dead already?

Dead is a strong term.
As for latest and greatest, it has been around for a year now. In computer
motherboard terms that is getting "pretty old".  ;^}

>I'm no alpha/linux wiz, is there a simple RH CD set I can literally
>install with one command (I'm not lazy, just dumb) and which will then
>work without me having to mother it? I can get access to the RH5.2
>all-processor CD set, but have been warned that "it comes with almost
